/* Lesson content – English.
   Keys are block indexes, so a missing entry can never shift the others. */
I18N.registerLessons("en", {

/* ===================== PIANO ===================== */
"piano-1":{title:"Meeting the keys",goal:"Identify any note on the piano within five seconds",b:{
0:"The piano looks intimidating: 88 keys. The truth? There are only <b>12 keys</b> that repeat over and over. The moment you see the pattern, the whole instrument opens up.",
1:"The secret is in the black keys: they are arranged in groups of <b>two</b> and <b>three</b>, alternating. That is your map.",
2:["Find a group of two black keys.","The white key immediately to their left (towards the low notes) is <b>C</b>.","From there just follow the alphabet: C, D, E, F, G, A, B — and then C again.","Every next C is the same sound, only higher. That is called an <b>octave</b>."],
3:"Press the highlighted key — this is C4. Try to find the other C's as well.",
4:"<b>Golden tip:</b> Middle C (C4) sits roughly in the centre of the piano, opposite the lock or the logo. It is every player's anchor point.",
5:"The C major scale — the seven white notes and back to C",
6:{q:"How do you find the note C on the piano?",o:["The white key to the left of three black keys","The white key right next to the group of two black keys, on the low side","The first black key","The white key in the middle of the keyboard"],w:"C is always the white key next to the group of two black keys, on the side of the lower notes."},
7:"Sit at the keyboard (or the on-screen one) and say out loud every C you can find, then every F. Three minutes and you are done."}},

"piano-2":{title:"The right hand — five fingers, five notes",goal:"Play C–D–E–F–G in sequence without looking at your hands",b:{
0:"On the piano every finger has a number: <b>1 = thumb</b>, 2 = index, 3 = middle, 4 = ring, 5 = little finger. The numbering is the same in both hands.",
1:"Place your right thumb on <b>C4</b> and each following finger on the next white key. Your five fingers now sit exactly on C-D-E-F-G. This is \"C position\" — the first hand position every pianist learns.",
2:"C position — fingers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5",
3:["Curve your fingers slightly, as if holding a small orange.","Keep the wrist level — neither sunken nor raised.","Play 1-2-3-4-5 slowly, each note at the same volume.","Now come back down: 5-4-3-2-1.","Once it flows, close your eyes and play it again."],
4:"<b>Even volume matters more than speed.</b> The weak finger (4) will always try to play softer. Give it extra attention.",
5:"Finger exercise 1",
6:{q:"Which finger is number 1 on the piano?",o:["The little finger","The middle finger","The thumb","The index finger"],w:"The thumb is always finger number 1 — in both hands."},
7:"Play the finger exercise five times in a row without a mistake. If you slip, start again more slowly."}},

"piano-3":{title:"Your first song",goal:"Play a complete melody by ear and from the page",b:{
0:"Here it is: you are playing a song. \"Twinkle, Twinkle\" is an 18th-century French folk melody (public domain) — Mozart even wrote variations on it.",
1:"Every note is inside the C position you learned, except <b>A4</b>, which we reach by moving the little finger slightly.",
2:"Twinkle, Twinkle — the complete melody",
3:"The notes you need for this song",
4:["Listen to the melody twice using the play button.","Play only the first four notes: C C G G.","Add: A A G (the G is long — two beats).","Continue: F F E E D D C.","Join it all slowly, and only then add speed."],
5:"<b>The key rule of self-teaching:</b> slow = fast. A brain that learns slowly and without mistakes reaches full speed three times quicker than one that rushes and stumbles.",
6:{q:"What is the right tempo to start a new song?",o:["As fast as possible","The tempo at which you play it without mistakes","The song's original tempo","It does not matter"],w:"The right tempo is the one where you succeed without errors. Speed arrives on its own afterwards."},
7:"Play Twinkle, Twinkle three times in a row without stopping."}},

"piano-4":{title:"Rhythm — the language of time",goal:"Count 1-2-3-4 and play different note values",b:{
0:"Music is not only which notes — it is <b>when</b>. The basic note values:",
1:["<b>Whole note</b> — lasts 4 beats.","<b>Half note</b> — 2 beats.","<b>Quarter note</b> — one beat. This is the most common note of all.","<b>Eighth note</b> — half a beat. You count 1-and-2-and."],
2:"Rhythm exercise — counting in four",
3:"Most popular songs have <b>four beats in a bar</b> (4/4). The first beat is stronger — it is the pulse of the song.",
4:"Clap first, play second. If you cannot clap a rhythm, you will not be able to play it.",
5:"Same note, different values",
6:{q:"How many beats does a quarter note last in 4/4?",o:["4","2","1","Half"],w:"A quarter note = one beat. Four quarters fill a complete bar."},
7:"Set the metronome to 70 and clap exactly on the beats. Then clap eighths (twice per beat)."}},

"piano-5":{title:"The left hand joins in",goal:"Play a basic bass line with the left hand",b:{
0:"The left hand plays the low notes — the harmonic foundation. We start simply: one note per bar.",
1:"C and G in the left-hand octave",
2:["Place your left little finger on C3.","Place your thumb on G3.","Play C — count 4. Play G — count 4.","Once that is steady, play C-G-C-G on beats 1 and 3."],
3:"The left hand always feels \"stupid\" at first. That is completely normal — it has simply had less practice in life. Within a week it catches up.",
4:"Bass exercise",
5:{q:"What is the left hand's main job in popular piano playing?",o:["Playing the melody","Playing the bass and harmony","Turning the pages","Keeping the beat only"],w:"The left hand builds the harmonic foundation that the right-hand melody sits on."},
6:"Play the bass exercise with the metronome at 70."}},

"piano-6":{title:"Both hands together — the big moment",goal:"Coordinate the hands in a simple exercise",b:{
0:"This is where most self-taught players get stuck, and for one reason: they try to combine the hands too soon.",
1:["Play the right hand alone until it is completely automatic.","Play the left hand alone until it is automatic.","Play just one bar with both hands — and stop.","Add one bar at a time.","If you get stuck, go back to hands separately. Always."],
2:"Two-hand exercise (the melody)",
3:"Two-hand exercise (the bass)",
4:"<b>A trick that works:</b> say the right-hand note names out loud while you play. It forces the brain to separate the hands.",
5:{q:"What should you do when you cannot combine both hands?",o:["Play faster","Give up on the left hand","Go back to hands separately and combine just one bar","Move to a different song"],w:"Breaking it into small pieces and rebuilding gradually is the only method that works."},
6:"Play both exercises together, two bars only, ten times."}},

"piano-7":{title:"C major scale and the thumb pass",goal:"Play a full octave with one hand",b:{
0:"You have only five fingers — and the scale has eight notes. The solution is the <b>thumb pass</b>, the single most important movement in piano technique.",
1:"C major scale ascending",
2:["Right hand: 1-2-3 on C-D-E.","While finger 3 plays E, the thumb \"passes\" underneath the palm.","The thumb lands on F — and from there 1-2-3-4-5 up to the next C.","The full fingering: 1 2 3 1 2 3 4 5.","Coming down: 5 4 3 2 1 3 2 1."],
3:"The elbow and forearm should move slightly to the right along with the hand. If only the thumb moves, it will feel clumsy.",
4:"C major scale — up and down",
5:{q:"When does the thumb pass under the palm in an ascending C major scale?",o:["After the note D","While finger 3 plays E","After G","It does not pass"],w:"The pass happens while finger 3 plays E, so the thumb lands on F in time."},
6:"Play the scale up and down four times at metronome 60."}},

"piano-8":{title:"Chords — three notes that change everything",goal:"Play C, F and G with the left hand",b:{
0:"A <b>chord</b> is three or more notes played together. A major chord is built by skipping: note, skip one, note, skip one, note.",
1:"C major chord — C-E-G",
2:"F major chord — F-A-C",
3:"G major chord — G-B-D",
4:"These three chords (C, F, G) are enough for hundreds of songs. They are the \"big three\" of the key.",
5:"Notice that every chord is built the same way: fingers 5-3-1 in the left hand, on a white key, skip a key, key, skip, key.",
6:{q:"Which notes make up a C major chord?",o:["C-D-E","C-E-G","C-F-A","C-G-C"],w:"A major chord is built from the first, third and fifth notes of the scale: C-E-G."},
7:"Move between C, F and G, four beats each, with the metronome at 60."}},

"piano-9":{title:"Accompaniment — playing a song with chords",goal:"Combine a right-hand melody with left-hand chords",b:{
0:"Now we join them: the left hand plays a chord, the right hand plays the melody. That is how every song in the world is accompanied.",
1:["Play \"Twinkle, Twinkle\" with the right hand.","In the first bar — chord C in the left hand.","When the melody reaches \"A A G\" — move to F and then to C.","In the bar before the end — G, and to finish, C again."],
2:"Melody to practise with",
3:"<b>How do you know which chord fits?</b> Check which chord contains the note the melody is playing on that beat. If the melody plays E, C will fit (C-E-G).",
4:{q:"Which chord contains the note A?",o:["C (C-E-G)","G (G-B-D)","F (F-A-C)","None of them"],w:"The F chord is made of F-A-C, so it contains the note A."},
5:"Play the song with accompaniment, very slowly, twice."}},

"piano-10":{title:"Major versus minor — the emotional colour",goal:"Recognise and play minor chords",b:{
0:"The difference between \"happy\" and \"sad\" in music is <b>one semitone</b>. Move the middle note of a major chord one key to the left and you have a minor chord.",
1:"A minor chord (Am) — A-C-E",
2:"D minor chord (Dm) — D-F-A",
3:"E minor chord (Em) — E-G-B",
4:"Play C and then Am one after the other. Notice how the mood of the sound changes instantly. That is the power of harmony.",
5:{q:"What is the difference between a major and a minor chord?",o:["Minor is higher","The middle note of a minor chord is a semitone lower","Minor has four notes","There is no real difference"],w:"The middle (third) note drops by a semitone — and that is the whole difference between \"happy\" and \"sad\"."},
6:"Play the sequence C – Am – F – G, four beats per chord. This is the most common progression in popular music."}},

"piano-11":{title:"Dynamics, pedal and expression",goal:"Turn technical playing into musical playing",b:{
0:"Two players will play the same notes — and one will sound like a robot. The difference is <b>dynamics</b>: changing the volume of your playing.",
1:["<b>Piano (p)</b> — soft. <b>Forte (f)</b> — loud.","<b>Crescendo</b> — getting gradually louder. <b>Diminuendo</b> — getting softer.","Play the scale: start soft, peak at the top C, and fade on the way down.","A musical phrase is like a spoken sentence — it has a beginning, a peak and an end."],
2:"<b>The right pedal</b> (the sustain pedal) lifts the dampers off the strings — and the sound keeps ringing. The rule: change the pedal at every chord change, otherwise you create mud.",
3:"The correct pedal technique: press the pedal <b>just after</b> the finger has played, not before. This is called \"late pedal\".",
4:{q:"When do you change the pedal?",o:["On every note","At every chord change","Only at the end of the piece","Never — you hold it down"],w:"Changing the pedal at every chord change keeps the sound clean."},
5:"Play the scale with a crescendo going up and a diminuendo coming down."}},

"piano-12":{title:"Final piece — Ode to Joy",goal:"Play a complete classical work",b:{
0:"You have finished the basic path. It is time to play a real piece: the theme from Beethoven's Ninth Symphony (1824) — public domain.",
1:"Ode to Joy",
2:["Play the right hand alone, four bars at a time.","Add a simple bass in the left hand: C – C – G – C.","Add dynamics: quiet at the start, a peak in the middle.","Add pedal at the chord changes.","Play from beginning to end without stopping — even if you make a mistake."],
3:"<b>The rule of an experienced player:</b> in performance you never stop to correct. You carry on. Corrections belong to practice, not to playing.",
4:{q:"What is the most effective way to practise a new piece?",o:["Playing it from start to finish over and over","Isolating the hard spots and practising only those","Playing only the easy parts","Just listening to recordings"],w:"Focused practice on the difficult spots saves hours. 80% of your time should go to the hardest 20%."},
5:"Record yourself playing (on your phone) and listen back. You will hear things you did not notice while playing."}},

/* ===================== GUITAR ===================== */
"guitar-1":{title:"Meeting the guitar and holding it right",goal:"Sit correctly, hold correctly and tune the guitar",b:{
0:"A guitar has six strings. The thickest (on top) is the <b>low E</b>, and the thinnest (at the bottom) is the <b>high E</b>. String numbering runs <b>from thin to thick</b>: the thinnest string is string 1.",
1:["String 6 — low E (E2)","String 5 — A (A2)","String 4 — D (D3)","String 3 — G (G3)","String 2 — B (B3)","String 1 — high E (E4)"],
2:"The sound of the open strings — thick to thin",
3:"<b>Sitting correctly:</b> back straight, the body of the guitar on your right leg (for a right-handed player), the neck angled slightly upward. Left-hand thumb behind the neck — not peeking over the top.",
4:"Sore fingertips in the first two weeks are normal — the skin hardens. Pain in the wrist or shoulder is <b>not</b> normal and means your posture is wrong. Stop and adjust how you are sitting.",
5:"Tune the guitar before every practice session. An out-of-tune guitar teaches your ear the wrong pitches. Use the <a href='#/tools/tuner'>tuner in the app</a>.",
6:{q:"Which string is string number 1?",o:["The thickest","The thinnest","The middle one","It depends on the guitar"],w:"String 1 is the thinnest — the high E."},
7:"Tune the guitar, then pluck each open string and say its name out loud."}},

"guitar-2":{title:"The right hand — your first strum",goal:"Produce a clean, even sound on open strings",b:{
0:"The right hand is responsible for sound and rhythm. You can strum with a pick (plectrum) or with your fingers — we will start with a pick.",
1:["Hold the pick between thumb and index finger, with only 3–5 mm sticking out.","The movement comes from the wrist, not the elbow.","Strum downward across all six strings — one continuous sound.","Now upward, from the thin string to the thick one.","Practise: down, down, down, down — at an even tempo."],
2:"Quarter-note strums — four beats",
3:"If the sound stutters, you are probably gripping the pick too tightly. A relaxed grip produces a smooth sound.",
4:{q:"Where should the strumming motion come from?",o:["The shoulder","The elbow","The wrist","The fingers only"],w:"A wrist movement is relaxed, fast and does not cause injuries."},
5:"Strum open strings for 2 continuous minutes with the metronome at 70. Focus on evenness of tone."}},

"guitar-3":{title:"Your first chord — Em",goal:"Play a clean chord and strum it in time",b:{
0:"Em (E minor) is the easiest chord in the world: only two fingers, and all six strings are played.",
1:null,
2:["Finger 2 on fret 2 of string 5 (A).","Finger 3 on fret 2 of string 4 (D).","Press close to the fret — not on it and not in the middle.","Fingers stand on their tips, they do not lie flat.","Pluck string by string and check that each one rings cleanly."],
3:"A \"dead\" or buzzing sound? Almost always the cause is a finger lying flat and touching a neighbouring string. Lift the palm and curve your fingers more.",
4:"The teacher's checking method: pluck each string separately (an \"arpeggio\"). A string that does not sound tells you exactly which finger to fix.",
5:{q:"What should you do when a string in a chord buzzes?",o:["Press harder with every finger","Check which finger is lying flat and touching a neighbouring string","Retune the guitar","Change the strings"],w:"In 90% of cases a neighbouring finger is touching the string. The fix is curving the fingers, not force."},
6:"Hold Em and strum 16 continuous beats. Release, shake your hand out, and repeat — five times."}},

"guitar-4":{title:"A second chord and your first change",goal:"Move between Em and Am without stopping",b:{
0:"Am (A minor) is exactly the same shape as Em — just moved over by one string, with an extra finger added.",
1:null,
2:["Take the Em shape and move it one string towards the thin strings.","Add finger 1 on fret 1 of string 2 (B).","Do not strum string 6.","Move Em → Am → Em → Am, without strumming — just the hand."],
3:"<b>The secret of chord changes:</b> practise the change <u>without strumming</u>, 20 times. The brain learns the movement without the pressure of sound. Then add the strum.",
4:"<b>Anchor finger:</b> between Em and Am two fingers keep the same shape. Do not lift the whole hand — slide it.",
5:{q:"What is the most effective way to practise a chord change?",o:["Playing the song fast over and over","Practising the hand movement alone, without strumming","Practising each chord separately","Practising only with a fast metronome"],w:"Separating the hand movement from the strumming lets the brain learn the motion cleanly and quickly."},
6:"60 changes Em↔Am. Count out loud. It is a four-minute exercise that changes everything."}},

"guitar-5":{title:"The strumming pattern — down and up",goal:"Play the world's most common accompaniment pattern",b:{
0:"The most basic pattern: <b>down, down-up, up-down-up</b>. If you only ever learn one strumming pattern in your life, make it this one.",
1:["Count out loud: 1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and.","The hand keeps moving down and up all the time — like a pendulum, never stopping.","On the beats you do not play, the hand keeps moving in the air, it simply does not touch.","The pattern: ↓ . ↓↑ . ↑↓↑ (1, 2-and, 3-and-4-and)."],
2:"The basic accompaniment pattern",
3:"The hand that never stops is the secret. Beginners stop the hand — and lose the beat. Let the hand always keep moving.",
4:{q:"Why should the right hand keep moving even when you are not strumming?",o:["It looks good","To keep a continuous sense of the beat","So the hand does not get tired","It does not matter"],w:"Continuous motion is your internal clock. Stopping breaks the beat."},
5:"Play Em with this pattern for a full minute without stopping, then Am."}},

"guitar-6":{title:"The C chord — and a tougher change",goal:"Play a clean C and use it in a sequence",b:{
0:"C (C major) needs three fingers on three different frets. This is the chord that separates those who persist from those who quit.",
1:null,
2:["Finger 1 — fret 1, string 2.","Finger 2 — fret 2, string 4.","Finger 3 — fret 3, string 5.","Do not strum string 6.","Make sure the open strings 1 and 3 ring freely."],
3:"If string 1 (the thin one) does not sound, your finger 1 is lying on it. Rotate your wrist slightly forward.",
4:{q:"Which strings are not played in an open C chord?",o:["String 1","String 6","Strings 5 and 6","No strings are left out"],w:"In a C chord you start from the fifth string. String 6 is not part of the chord."},
5:"Practise C → Am → C → Am, four beats each."}},

"guitar-7":{title:"G and D — your first three-chord song",goal:"Play a complete song with G, C and D",b:{
0:"With G, C and D you can play thousands of songs. This is the \"G family\" — the three central chords of the key of G major.",
1:null,
2:null,
3:"<b>The classic structure:</b> G – G – C – C – G – G – D – C – G. Four beats per chord.",
4:"Accompaniment pattern for the song",
5:"Notice that moving from G to C, finger 3 can stay on the same fret — it is an \"anchor finger\" that halves the length of the change.",
6:{q:"What do the three chords G, C and D have in common?",o:["They are all minor","They are the three central chords of the G major scale","They are on the same fret","They use the same fingers"],w:"These are degrees I, IV and V of the G major scale — the harmonic skeleton of most songs."},
7:"Play the cycle G-C-G-D twice without stopping, even if the changes are slow."}},

"guitar-8":{title:"Fast changes and anchor fingers",goal:"Shorten the time between chords",b:{
0:"Slow chord changes are the number one problem for beginner guitarists. The solution is not strength or speed — it is <b>efficiency</b>.",
1:["<b>Anchor finger:</b> spot a finger that stays in the same place between two chords — do not lift it.","<b>Minimal movement:</b> lift your fingers only 2 mm off the strings.","<b>Timing:</b> change chord on the last beat, not after it.","<b>The clock exercise:</b> set one minute and count how many clean changes you manage. Beat your record every day."],
2:"Write your change record in the app's practice journal. Measurable improvement is the strongest engine of persistence.",
3:{q:"What is an \"anchor finger\"?",o:["The thumb behind the neck","A finger that stays in place when moving between two chords","The strongest finger","A finger that presses two strings"],w:"An anchor finger shortens the change and gives the other fingers a reference point."},
4:"One minute of C↔G changes. Write down the number. Come back tomorrow and beat it."}},

"guitar-9":{title:"Patterns — varying your accompaniment",goal:"Learn three different strumming patterns",b:{
0:"The same chords with a different pattern sound like a completely different song. Here are three patterns that cover almost any style.",
1:"Pattern 1 — slow ballad",
2:"Pattern 2 — standard pop",
3:"Pattern 3 — off-beat accent pattern",
4:"<b>The off-beat pattern</b> — strumming only on the \"and\" between beats — gives a bouncy feel and suits reggae, ska and many folk styles.",
5:{q:"What changes when you play the same chords with a different pattern?",o:["The harmony","The character and style of the song","The pitch","Only the speed"],w:"The pattern is what defines the style. The same four chords can sound like a ballad, a rock song or reggae."},
6:"Play the same chord sequence with all three patterns."}},

"guitar-10":{title:"Your first solo — the pentatonic scale",goal:"Improvise a short solo on five notes",b:{
0:"The pentatonic scale (five notes) is the secret of every rock and blues soloist. Its magic: it is almost impossible to play a \"wrong\" note.",
1:"A minor pentatonic — ascending",
2:["Put finger 1 on fret 5, string 6 (A).","The shape: 5-8 on strings 6 and 5, 5-7 on strings 4 and 3, 5-8 on strings 2 and 1.","Play the shape up and down until your fingers remember it.","Now forget the order — play the notes in a random order. That is improvisation."],
3:"A good solo is not many notes — it is notes with <b>silences</b> between them. Leave room to breathe.",
4:{q:"Why is the pentatonic scale easy to improvise with?",o:["It only has high notes","It contains 5 notes that all fit harmonically","You can play it on one string only","It is played slowly"],w:"The \"clashing\" notes have been removed from the scale — so any combination of them sounds good."},
5:"Improvise 8 bars in the pentatonic scale over a metronome."}},

"guitar-11":{title:"Chord families — A, E, Dm",goal:"Expand your chord vocabulary to eight",b:{
0:null,1:null,2:null,
3:"With the eight chords you now know (Em, Am, C, G, D, A, E, Dm) you can play the vast majority of popular songs.",
4:"<b>The matching rule:</b> chords from the same \"family\" (key) sound good together. Key of C: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am. Key of G: G, Am, Bm, C, D, Em.",
5:{q:"Which chord does not belong to the G major family?",o:["C","D","Em","F"],w:"F is not part of the G major scale (which has an F sharp). It belongs to the C family."},
6:"Choose four chords from one family and play them in a loop."}},

"guitar-12":{title:"Final piece and a look ahead — barre chords",goal:"Play a complete song and meet the next stage",b:{
0:"You have finished the basic path. You can tune, strum, play eight chords, three patterns and a solo.",
1:"A closing melody — Amazing Grace (public domain)",
2:"<b>The next stage: the barre chord.</b> Finger 1 lies across all the strings and imitates a capo. This opens up the entire neck of the guitar.",
3:["Lay finger 1 flat across all six strings at fret 1.","Use the outer side of the finger, not the soft pad.","Add the E shape with fingers 2, 3 and 4 — and you have an F.","Do not despair: a clean barre takes most learners 2–4 weeks."],
4:"If you feel pain in your wrist while playing a barre — stop. The problem is the angle of your thumb, not a lack of strength. Practise at fret 5, where the strings are softer.",
5:{q:"What is a barre chord?",o:["A chord played only on open strings","A chord where one finger presses several strings at once","A chord with four notes","A fast chord"],w:"Finger 1 creates a \"movable nut\", letting you move chord shapes anywhere on the neck."},
6:"Play a complete song from the song library with chord accompaniment and record yourself."}},

/* ===================== UKULELE ===================== */
"ukulele-1":{title:"Ukulele in 10 minutes",goal:"Play your first chord and strum it",b:{
0:"The ukulele is the fastest instrument in the world to start on: four soft nylon strings and a standard tuning of <b>G-C-E-A</b>.",
1:"The open strings",
2:null,
3:["Hold the ukulele against your body with your right forearm.","Place finger 3 on fret 3 of the thin string (A).","Strum gently downward with the pad of your index finger.","That's it — you have played a C chord."],
4:"On the ukulele you strum around the point where the neck meets the body — that is where the tone is fullest.",
5:{q:"What is the standard tuning of a soprano ukulele?",o:["E-A-D-G","G-C-E-A","C-F-A-D","D-G-B-E"],w:"G-C-E-A is the standard tuning, and the G is actually higher than the C (\"high G tuning\")."},
6:"Strum the C chord for a minute at an even tempo."}},

"ukulele-2":{title:"Two chords = a whole song",goal:"Move between C and Am",b:{
0:null,
1:"Am also needs only one finger — finger 2 on fret 2 of the thick string (G).",
2:"Simple pattern — four downstrokes",
3:"Play four beats of C, four beats of Am, and repeat. That is already a legitimate song form you can sing over.",
4:{q:"How many fingers does the Am chord need on the ukulele?",o:["One","Two","Three","Four"],w:"Just one finger, on fret 2 of the thick string."},
5:"30 changes C↔Am with a slow metronome."}},

"ukulele-3":{title:"The third chord — F",goal:"Play a three-chord sequence",b:{
0:null,
1:"With C, Am and F you can already play hundreds of songs. The sequence C – Am – F – C is a classic.",
2:"Pop pattern",
3:{q:"What is a common progression using these three chords?",o:["F – F – F – F","C – Am – F – C","C – C# – D","Am only"],w:"C – Am – F is one of the most frequent progressions in popular music."},
4:"Play the sequence C-Am-F-C four times in a row."}},

"ukulele-4":{title:"Patterns and how to vary them",goal:"Three accompaniment patterns",b:{
0:"Island pattern",
1:"Waltz — three beats",
2:"The \"island\" pattern (down, down-up, up-down-up) is the classic ukulele sound.",
3:"Keep your right hand completely relaxed. On the ukulele, force equals a harsh tone.",
4:{q:"How many beats are there in a bar of waltz time?",o:["2","3","4","6"],w:"A waltz is in 3/4 — three beats per bar, with the accent on the first."},
5:"Play the sequence you learned using two different patterns."}},

"ukulele-5":{title:"G and Em — opening up the repertoire",goal:"Five chords under control",b:{
0:null,1:null,
2:"G needs three fingers in a triangle, and Em is a descending diagonal. Both need slow practice at first.",
3:"Practise the change C→G without strumming, 20 times. You will notice your hand \"learns\" the shape within minutes.",
4:{q:"What helps most in mastering a new chord change?",o:["Playing fast","Practising the movement without strumming","Pressing harder","Changing the strings"],w:"Separating movement from sound speeds up motor learning."},
5:"30 changes C↔G and 30 changes Am↔Em."}},

"ukulele-6":{title:"Fingerpicking",goal:"Play a basic arpeggio",b:{
0:"Instead of strumming all the strings together, we will pluck them one after another — this is called an <b>arpeggio</b> and suits quiet songs.",
1:["Thumb — string 4 (G).","Index — string 3 (C).","Middle — string 2 (E).","Ring — string 1 (A).","Play 4-3-2-1 and then 1-2-3-4."],
2:"Arpeggio on the C chord",
3:{q:"What is an arpeggio?",o:["A hard strum","Playing the notes of a chord one after another","A fast chord change","A type of pick"],w:"Arpeggio = \"broken chord\" — the notes are played in sequence rather than together."},
4:"Play an arpeggio on C, Am and F, four beats each."}},

"ukulele-7":{title:"Melody on the ukulele",goal:"Play a melody, not just accompaniment",b:{
0:"The ukulele can play melodies too. We will use the C, E and A strings.",
1:"Twinkle, Twinkle — melody",
2:"Mark the notes on the neck with small stickers at first — it is a completely legitimate shortcut.",
3:{q:"What is the benefit of playing melodies on your instrument?",o:["It is easier than chords","It trains your ear and your knowledge of where each note lives","It sounds louder","There is no benefit"],w:"Playing melody connects your ear to the fretboard — a skill nothing else replaces."},
4:"Play the melody twice, slowly."}},

"ukulele-8":{title:"Singing and playing at the same time",goal:"Combine accompaniment with singing",b:{
0:"This is the skill that makes you the person invited to play at the campfire. The secret is <b>automation</b>: the hand must play without you thinking about it.",
1:["Play the accompaniment until you can hold a conversation while doing it.","Say the words in rhythm, without singing.","Hum the melody while you play.","Only now — sing words and melody together."],
2:"If the singing \"drags\" your hand, stop singing, steady the hand, and try again. The hand is the anchor.",
3:{q:"What is the condition for singing and playing at the same time?",o:["A good voice","That the accompaniment is completely automatic","Complicated chords","A metronome"],w:"Automatic accompaniment frees your attention for the singing."},
4:"Play accompaniment and hum a melody for one continuous minute."}},

"ukulele-9":{title:"Final piece and a look ahead",goal:"Play a complete song from beginning to end",b:{
0:"Hava Nagila — traditional melody (public domain)",
1:"The next steps on the ukulele: barre chords, the \"chunk\" technique (rhythmic muting), and advanced fingerpicking.",
2:"Find a friend to play with. Playing together improves your timing far more than any amount of practice alone.",
3:{q:"What is the recommended next step after mastering open chords?",o:["Change instrument","Barre chords and advanced patterns","Stop practising","Learn notation only"],w:"The barre opens up the whole neck and lets you play in any key."},
4:"Record yourself playing a complete song."}},

/* ===================== RECORDER ===================== */
"recorder-1":{title:"Breath and your first fingering",goal:"Produce a clean tone and play B",b:{
0:"The recorder is the perfect instrument to start with: cheap, light, and it demands no strength or large hands.",
1:["Hold the recorder at about 45 degrees from your body.","Left hand on top — always! This is the most common mistake.","Rest the tip of the mouthpiece on your lower lip and close your lips gently — without biting.","Blow gently and whisper \"too\" — this is the tonguing that starts every note."],
2:"Blowing too hard will give a shrill, sharp sound. The recorder needs <b>very little</b> air. Less is more.",
3:"The note B",
4:"Your first note: the left thumb closes the back hole, the left index finger closes the top hole. That's it — that is B.",
5:{q:"Which hand is always on top on the recorder?",o:["Right","Left","It depends whether you are left-handed","It does not matter"],w:"The left hand is always on top — on every woodwind instrument."},
6:"Play 10 long, steady B notes, each lasting 4 seconds."}},

"recorder-2":{title:"Three notes — B, A, G",goal:"Play a short melody",b:{
0:["<b>B</b> — thumb + left finger 1.","<b>A</b> — add left finger 2.","<b>G</b> — add left finger 3."],
1:"Three-note exercise",
2:"Make sure your fingers cover the holes <b>completely</b> — with the pad of the finger, not the tip. A half-covered hole means a shrieking sound.",
3:{q:"What happens when a hole is not fully covered?",o:["The note is too low","The note shrieks or whistles","Nothing happens","The recorder breaks"],w:"Air leaking through a half-covered hole creates a whistle."},
4:"Play the exercise five times without any squeaks."}},

"recorder-3":{title:"Your first song",goal:"Play a familiar melody",b:{
0:"Mary Had a Little Lamb — folk melody (public domain)",
1:"This song uses the notes you have already learned plus C and D — you add the right-hand fingers.",
2:"Hum the melody first, and only then play it on the instrument. An ear that knows the tune guides the fingers.",
3:{q:"What is the recommended step before playing a new song?",o:["Playing it fast","Humming it and getting to know the melody","Reading the notes aloud","Tuning the instrument"],w:"An ear that knows the melody spots a mistake instantly."},
4:"Play the whole song twice."}},

"recorder-4":{title:"Tonguing and articulation",goal:"Separate notes cleanly",b:{
0:"Without tonguing, all the notes merge into one long sound. The tongue is the punctuation of your playing.",
1:["<b>\"Too\"</b> — a sharp start to each note.","<b>\"Doo\"</b> — a soft start, for flowing passages.","<b>Legato</b> — tongue only the first note of a group.","<b>Staccato</b> — short, separated notes."],
2:"Staccato versus legato",
3:{q:"What is the tongue used for when playing the recorder?",o:["Keeping the note high","Separating and starting notes cleanly","Making the sound louder","Tuning the instrument"],w:"The tongue defines the beginning of every note — without it, playing sounds smeared."},
4:"Play a full scale once staccato and once legato."}},

"recorder-5":{title:"The full C major scale",goal:"Play a complete octave",b:{
0:"C major scale",
1:"A full scale needs cooperation between both hands. The main difficulty: moving from left-hand notes to right-hand notes.",
2:"Practise only the problem transition (G→F) twenty times, instead of playing the whole scale over and over.",
3:{q:"What is better when a passage is hard?",o:["Playing the whole passage repeatedly","Isolating the two problem notes and practising only those","Skipping the passage","Slowing down the whole song"],w:"Focused practice is many times more effective than repeating the whole."},
4:"Play the scale up and down 5 times at metronome 60."}},

"recorder-6":{title:"The second octave",goal:"Produce high notes using the thumb",b:{
0:"To go up an octave, the thumb \"pinches\" the back hole — opening it just a fraction. This is the <b>overblowing</b> technique.",
1:["Slide your thumb so the nail opens a small gap.","Increase the air pressure slightly.","Play low G and then high G alternately."],
2:"Octaves",
3:"Shrieking sound? The gap is too big. Low sound? The gap is closed. The difference is a millimetre.",
4:{q:"What is the role of the left thumb on high notes?",o:["Closing completely","Opening a small gap","Only holding the instrument","Opening completely"],w:"A small gap changes the vibration mode and raises the note by an octave."},
5:"Play the octave exercise 10 times."}},

"recorder-7":{title:"Sharps and flats",goal:"Play semitones",b:{
0:"On the recorder you produce semitones with <b>fork fingerings</b> — covering non-consecutive holes.",
1:"F sharp (F#) is the most important note to learn — it is needed in every song in the key of G major.",
2:"G major scale (with F#)",
3:{q:"What is a fork fingering?",o:["Covering all the holes","Covering non-consecutive holes to create a semitone","Opening all the holes","A fingering using both hands"],w:"Skipping a hole in the middle of the fingering lowers the note by a semitone."},
4:"Play the G major scale up and down."}},

"recorder-8":{title:"Expression, breathing and musical phrasing",goal:"Play musically rather than mechanically",b:{
0:"Breathing is not only a physical need — it is punctuation. You breathe at the end of a musical phrase, not in the middle of a word.",
1:["Mark in the music where you will breathe — before you start playing.","Breathe through your mouth, quickly and quietly.","Do not breathe in the middle of a long note.","End a phrase with a gentle softening."],
2:"Play a musical phrase as if you were speaking a sentence: a beginning, a peak, and a softened ending.",
3:{q:"Where is the right place to breathe?",o:["Every two beats","At the end of a musical phrase","Whenever you run out of air","In the middle of a long note"],w:"A planned breath at the end of a phrase keeps the musical line intact."},
4:"Play a complete song and mark three breathing points in advance."}},

"recorder-9":{title:"Final piece — Ode to Joy",goal:"Play a complete classical work",b:{
0:"Ode to Joy — Beethoven (public domain)",
1:"The recorder is an excellent gateway to other wind instruments: flute, clarinet, saxophone. Breathing and tonguing transfer almost unchanged.",
2:{q:"Which instrument is considered a natural continuation after the recorder?",o:["Drums","Flute or clarinet","Piano","Violin"],w:"The breathing and tonguing technique transfers directly to woodwind instruments."},
3:"Play the piece from beginning to end without stopping."}},

/* ===================== DRUMS ===================== */
"drums-1":{title:"Rhythm — the body language of music",goal:"Keep a steady pulse",b:{
0:"You do not need a drum kit to start. A table, two hands or two pencils are perfectly enough for the first three lessons.",
1:"Quarter-note beats — counting in four",
2:["Set the metronome to 72.","Clap exactly on every beat.","Count out loud: 1-2-3-4.","Accent the first beat."],
3:"<b>The test:</b> if you are rushing or dragging, you hear the metronome \"moving\". If you are accurate, it almost disappears underneath your clap.",
4:{q:"What is a sign that you are accurate with the metronome?",o:["The metronome sounds louder","The metronome almost disappears underneath your sound","You are playing fast","There is no way to tell"],w:"When your hit lands exactly on the click, the two sounds merge."},
5:"Two minutes of accurate clapping at 72."}},

"drums-2":{title:"The basic groove",goal:"Play the groove found in 80% of songs",b:{
0:"The basic groove has three components: <b>hi-hat</b> (continuous eighths), <b>bass drum</b> (beats 1 and 3), <b>snare</b> (beats 2 and 4).",
1:null,
2:["Start with the hi-hat alone: 1-and-2-and-3-and-4-and.","Add the bass drum on beats 1 and 3.","Add the snare on beats 2 and 4.","Play all three together — slowly."],
3:"If one hand or foot \"knocks over\" the others, drop the speed by 20%. Speed is a result, not a goal.",
4:{q:"On which beats does the snare fall in the basic groove?",o:["1 and 3","2 and 4","Every beat","Only on 1"],w:"The snare on 2 and 4 is the \"backbeat\" — the pulse of rock and pop."},
5:"Play the groove for 16 continuous bars at 70."}},

"drums-3":{title:"Limb independence",goal:"Separate the hands from the feet",b:{
0:"Independence is the ability to have each limb doing something different at the same time. It is learned — nobody is born with it.",
1:["Play hi-hat and snare only.","Play hi-hat and bass drum only.","Play bass drum and snare only.","Combine all three.","Move the bass drum to the \"4-and\" — and feel your brain fight it. That is exactly the training."],
2:"Independence exercise",
3:"Practise independence for 5 minutes a day — better than an hour once a week. It is motor learning, and it needs spaced repetition.",
4:{q:"What is the effective way to develop independence?",o:["Playing fast","Breaking it into pairs of limbs and combining gradually","Playing complete songs","Listening to music"],w:"Breaking it into pairs lets the brain build the pattern step by step."},
5:"Practise three pairs of limbs, one minute each."}},

"drums-4":{title:"Fills — the transition between sections",goal:"Play a one-bar fill",b:{
0:"A <b>fill</b> is a short passage that marks a transition between sections of a song. The basic fill: four pairs of eighths across snare and toms.",
1:"A one-bar fill",
2:"The most important rule of a fill: <b>come back in time</b>. An impressive fill that lands late is worse than a simple one that lands exactly on the beat.",
3:{q:"What is the most important thing about a fill?",o:["That it is fast","That it ends exactly in time for beat 1","That it is long","That it uses every drum"],w:"The fill serves the song — landing accurately matters more than the content."},
4:"Play 4 bars of groove + one bar of fill, four times."}},

"drums-5":{title:"Dynamics and weight",goal:"Control the volume of your strokes",b:{
0:"A good drummer does not hit hard — they hit <b>right</b>. A good groove contains at least three levels of volume.",
1:["Hi-hat — soft and even.","Bass drum — medium, felt in the stomach.","Snare — loud and sharp; this is what people hear.","Try playing the same groove at 30% volume and then at 90%."],
2:"Volume control comes from the grip: a relaxed hold with a \"fulcrum point\" between thumb and finger gives a free, bouncing stroke.",
3:{q:"Which element of the groove should be the loudest?",o:["The hi-hat","The snare","All at equal volume","The bass drum"],w:"The snare is the \"click\" of the groove and therefore stands out."},
4:"Play the groove at three different volume levels."}},

"drums-6":{title:"Grooves in different styles",goal:"Learn four grooves",b:{
0:"Rock",1:"Shuffle / blues",2:"Waltz",3:"Funk (sixteenths)",
4:"Style is not only a pattern — it is also a feel. A shuffle swings, rock is straight, funk is sharp and choppy.",
5:{q:"What distinguishes rhythmic styles from one another?",o:["Only the speed","The pattern of strokes and the feel (straight versus swung)","The type of drums","The playing volume"],w:"The pattern and the groove feel are what define the style."},
6:"Play each of the four grooves for 8 bars."}},

"drums-7":{title:"Reading drum notation",goal:"Decode a groove chart",b:{
0:"Drum notation uses the same staff, but each line represents a different drum: hi-hat on top (X), snare in the middle, bass drum at the bottom.",
1:["Top line with an X — hi-hat.","The third space — snare.","The bottom space — bass drum.","Count the bar: 1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and, and mark what falls where."],
2:{q:"What does an X mean in drum notation?",o:["Bass drum","A cymbal or hi-hat","Snare","A rest"],w:"An X-shaped notehead indicates a metal instrument — hi-hat, crash or ride."},
3:"Write the basic groove out on paper in notation."}},

"drums-8":{title:"Playing along with a song",goal:"Hold the beat together with music",b:{
0:"The drummer is the band's clock. To come in: find beat 1, count a full bar, and enter.",
1:["Listen to the song and find the backbeat (2 and 4).","Clap along with it for 30 seconds.","Play the basic groove over the song.","Add a fill at the end of every 8 bars."],
2:"Do not fill every second. A drummer's silences matter exactly as much as the strokes.",
3:{q:"What is the drummer's central role in a band?",o:["Being the loudest","Keeping a steady beat and leading the transitions","Playing solos","Changing chords"],w:"Steady timing is the foundation the whole band leans on."},
4:"Play along with a song you love for 3 minutes."}},

"drums-9":{title:"Wrap-up and a look ahead",goal:"Combine groove, fills and dynamics",b:{
0:"You have finished the fundamentals: groove, independence, fills, dynamics and styles.",
1:["Play 8 bars of rock groove.","A one-bar fill.","8 bars of shuffle.","A fill.","Finish with a crash on beat 1."],
2:"The next stage: rudiments (paradiddle, ruff, double stroke roll) — these are the drummer's \"scales\".",
3:{q:"What are rudiments?",o:["Types of drums","Basic stroke patterns that everything is built from","Latin rhythms","Types of sticks"],w:"Rudiments are the building blocks of drumming technique."},
4:"Record yourself playing a complete sequence."}},

/* ===================== BASS ===================== */
"bass-1":{title:"Meeting the bass",goal:"Play open strings in time",b:{
0:"The bass guitar is the bridge between the drums and the guitar. Four strings: <b>E-A-D-G</b>, an octave below the four thick strings of a guitar.",
1:"The open strings",
2:["Right hand: index and middle fingers alternate — the \"two-finger technique\".","The thumb rests on the pickup.","Left hand: one finger per fret.","Play each open string four times."],
3:"<b>The bassist's first rule:</b> one clean, short note in the right place is worth more than ten impressive ones.",
4:{q:"What is the standard tuning of a four-string bass?",o:["G-C-E-A","E-A-D-G","E-A-D-G-B-E","D-A-D-G"],w:"E-A-D-G — the same as the four thick strings of a guitar, one octave lower."},
5:"Play 8 beats on each open string with the metronome at 70."}},

"bass-2":{title:"Your first bass line",goal:"Play the root of each chord",b:{
0:"The bassist's basic job: play the <b>root note</b> of the chord the guitar is playing, on the first beat of every bar.",
1:"Bass line for the sequence G-C-D",
2:["Chord G → play G.","Chord C → play C.","Chord D → play D.","One long beat per bar, then four quarter notes."],
3:"A good bassist knows where every note is without thinking. Practise: \"where is F?\" and find it in three different places.",
4:{q:"What is the root note?",o:["The highest note of the chord","The note that gives the chord its name","The first note of the scale","The thick string"],w:"For a G chord the root is G. That is the bassist's fundamental note."},
5:"Play roots for the sequence G-C-D-G, four beats each."}},

"bass-3":{title:"Rhythm and groove with the drums",goal:"Lock the bass in with the kick drum",b:{
0:"The magic of a band happens when the bass and the bass drum play <b>exactly together</b>. This is called \"the lock\".",
1:"Kick drum pattern to practise against",
2:["Listen to the kick drum pattern.","Play one note exactly on every kick.","Do not add notes — just be accurate.","Now add a passing note before the next bar."],
3:"If you are playing between the beats and you are not sure — do not play. An accurate silence is better than an inaccurate note.",
4:{q:"Which instrument should the bass be most synchronised with?",o:["The guitar","The bass drum","The vocals","The piano"],w:"Bass plus kick drum is the rhythmic foundation of every band."},
5:"Play along with the kick drum pattern for two minutes."}},

"bass-4":{title:"Notes on the neck",goal:"Find any note in two places",b:{
0:"On the E string: fret 1 = F, fret 3 = G, fret 5 = A. On the A string: fret 2 = B, fret 3 = C.",
1:"<b>The octave rule:</b> the same note is also two strings down and two frets along. It is the most useful shortcut on the bass.",
2:"The same note in two octaves",
3:{q:"Where is the octave of a note on the bass?",o:["On the same string 12 frets up, or two strings down and two frets along","On the same fret on the neighbouring string","There is no fixed rule","Three frets back"],w:"Both are correct, and the \"two and two\" shape is the quick one to use."},
4:"Pick a note and find it in three different places on the neck."}},

"bass-5":{title:"Walking bass",goal:"Connect chords with passing notes",b:{
0:"<b>Walking bass</b> — a bass line that plays four notes per bar and steps from one chord to the next.",
1:"A simple walking line",
2:["Start on the root.","Play notes from the chord (root, third, fifth).","Reach a note one step away from the next root.","Land on the next root on beat 1."],
3:{q:"What is the purpose of a passing note in a walking bass line?",o:["Filling space","Leading smoothly to the root of the next chord","Sounding fast","Replacing the drums"],w:"A passing note creates expectation and a natural pull towards the next chord."},
4:"Play a walking line over the sequence C-F-G-C."}},

"bass-6":{title:"Right-hand techniques",goal:"Meet fingerstyle, pick and slap",b:{
0:["<b>Fingerstyle:</b> the warmest and most common tone.","<b>Pick:</b> a sharp, cutting sound, good for rock and punk.","<b>Slap:</b> the thumb strikes the thick string, the finger \"pops\" the thin one — the characteristic funk sound."],
1:"Basic slap pattern",
2:"In slap, the thumb rotates around an axis — it does not \"stab\". The bounce back is part of the movement.",
3:{q:"What is the slap technique?",o:["Playing with a pick","A thumb strike and a finger pop","Playing with both hands","Muting strings"],w:"The combination of thumb strike and finger pop creates the percussive sound."},
4:"Play eight bars with each of the techniques."}},

"bass-7":{title:"Muting and noise control",goal:"Play cleanly without ringing strings",b:{
0:"The number one problem on bass is not wrong notes — it is <b>noise</b>: strings that carry on vibrating.",
1:["The right-hand thumb rests on the thick string and mutes it.","The left-hand fingers that are not playing touch neighbouring strings lightly.","After every note, think about how you <b>stop</b> it, not only how you start it.","Play a note, mute it completely, and only then play the next one."],
2:{q:"What is the most common problem in bass playing?",o:["Wrong notes","Noise from strings that were not muted","Slow tempo","Thin strings"],w:"Noise control is the difference between an amateur and a professional."},
3:"Play note-silence-note-silence for one minute."}},

"bass-8":{title:"A bass line for a whole song",goal:"Build an independent bass line",b:{
0:"A good bass line is built in three stages: roots only → roots in rhythm → adding passing notes and variation.",
1:"Blues riff (original exercise)",
2:"Listen to the bassists in songs you love. Notice how little they play — and how right it feels.",
3:{q:"What is the correct order for building a bass line?",o:["Improvise straight away","Roots → rhythm → passing notes","Copy a solo","Start from the highest note"],w:"Building gradually keeps the line steady and in service of the song."},
4:"Build a bass line for a song you know, using the three stages."}},

"bass-9":{title:"Wrap-up and a look ahead",goal:"Play with a group",b:{
0:"You have finished the fundamentals. A bassist with steady timing, a clean tone and harmonic understanding is wanted in every band.",
1:["Practise with a metronome every day, even for 5 minutes.","Record yourself — the ear catches what the hand does not feel.","Play with other people as soon as you possibly can.","Learn the major scale and the natural minor across the whole neck."],
2:{q:"What is the most important quality in a bassist?",o:["Speed","Steady timing and a clean tone","Knowledge of notation","Expensive gear"],w:"Steadiness is what makes the whole band sound good."},
3:"Play a complete song from beginning to end with a metronome."}}

});

/* ---- ארבעת הכלים החדשים ---- */
I18N.registerLessons("en", {

/* ===================== VIOLIN ===================== */
"violin-1":{title:"Holding it, and the open strings",goal:"Produce a clean tone on all four strings",b:{
0:"The violin is the instrument where <b>your ear leads your finger</b>. There are no frets, so every note is built by listening — and that is exactly what makes it the best teacher an ear can have.",
1:"Four strings, thickest to thinnest: <b>G, D, A, E</b>. The distance between each pair is a fifth.",
3:["Rest the violin on your collarbone, with your jaw on the chinrest.","Your left hand holds the neck lightly — it does not grip.","The bow sits on the string halfway between the bridge and the fingerboard.","Draw the bow slowly with even pressure. A clean tone matters more than a loud one."],
4:"A whistling sound? One of three things: uneven bow pressure, the bow too far from the bridge, or changing speed. Slow down.",
5:{q:"How are the violin strings tuned?",o:["In fourths","In fifths","In octaves","In thirds"],w:"G-D-A-E — a fifth between each pair of neighbouring strings."},
6:"Play each open string eight times with a full bow, with no whistling."}},

"violin-2":{title:"The first finger",goal:"Play four new notes",b:{
0:"The first finger lands a <b>whole tone</b> away from the open string. This is the anchor point for the whole hand.",
2:"Stick a small piece of tape where the first finger lands. That is not cheating — it is how every violinist in the world learns in their first year.",
3:"Open string and first finger",
4:{q:"How far is the first finger from the open string?",o:["A semitone","A whole tone","A tone and a half","Two tones"],w:"A whole tone — and it is the first distance the ear learns."},
5:"Play the exercise slowly, and check every note against the tuner in the app."}},

"violin-3":{title:"A first melody",goal:"Play a whole tune on two strings",b:{
0:"With the open strings and the first finger you can already play a real melody.",
2:"Two-string exercise",
3:"<b>The rule of the first year:</b> slow and clean beats fast and messy, every single time.",
4:{q:"What matters most at this stage?",o:["Playing fast to make progress","Playing slowly and listening to every note","Skipping the hard notes","Playing as loudly as possible"],w:"On a violin every inaccurate note is heard immediately. Slowing down is the shortcut."},
5:"Play the melody five times, a little faster each time."}},

"violin-4":{title:"Three fingers — your first scale",goal:"Play a D major scale across two strings",b:{
0:"On a violin a scale is not built from frets but from a <b>fixed pattern of fingers</b>. Once the hand learns it, it repeats on every string.",
1:"On the D string: finger 1 = E, finger 2 = F sharp, finger 3 = G. Notice that fingers 2 and 3 <b>touch each other</b> — that is the sign the pattern is right.",
3:"D major scale",
4:"Fingers 2 and 3 are together in a major scale. If a gap opens between them, the F sharp will come out flat.",
5:{q:"What is special about fingers 2 and 3 in a major scale?",o:["A wide gap between them","They touch each other","Finger 3 is unused","There is no fixed rule"],w:"Their closeness is the semitone between the third and fourth notes of the scale."},
6:"Play the scale up and down ten times, slowly, checking each note with the tuner."}},

"violin-5":{title:"Dividing the bow",goal:"Control the length and the strength of a note",b:{
0:"The bow is the <b>breath</b> of the violin. A long note needs a slow bow, a short note needs little bow — and the pressure stays constant.",
1:["Mark three areas for yourself: frog, middle, tip.","Play a whole note with a full bow, frog to tip, in four seconds.","Play four quarter notes in that same bow — each one a quarter of the length.","Now play eighth notes using only the middle of the bow."],
2:"Dividing the bow",
3:"Tone fading at the end of the bow? The pressure drops along with your arm. Keep it even all the way to the tip.",
4:{q:"What determines the length of a note on the violin?",o:["Finger pressure","Bow speed","How you hold the instrument","String thickness"],w:"Bow speed sets the length; pressure sets the volume."},
5:"Play a full scale with a whole bow per note, then again with half a bow."}},

"violin-6":{title:"A second string, and crossings",goal:"Cross between strings without noise",b:{
0:"String crossings are where you hear the difference between a beginner and a player. The secret: <b>the arm starts moving before the note changes</b>.",
2:"Crossing exercise",
3:"If you hear a double sound during the crossing, the bow touched two strings. The angle should change, not the pressure.",
4:{q:"What causes a double sound when crossing strings?",o:["Too much pressure","The bow is touching two strings","The instrument is out of tune","The finger is in the wrong place"],w:"It is the bow angle that separates the strings."},
5:"Play the crossing exercise ten times, completely clean."}},

"violin-7":{title:"A first vibrato",goal:"Learn the vibrato motion",b:{
0:"Vibrato is a <b>small rocking of the finger</b> that warms the tone. It is taught later than people expect — and that is deliberate: it hides inaccuracy, so first you learn to play cleanly without it.",
1:["Place finger 2 on the A string, without the bow.","Rock your hand back and forth from the wrist, not from the finger.","The finger stays in place — only the pitch shifts slightly.","Add the bow only once the motion is smooth."],
2:"A fast, narrow vibrato sounds like a tremble. A slow, wide one sounds like singing. Start slow.",
3:{q:"Where does the vibrato motion come from?",o:["The finger","The wrist","The elbow only","The shoulder"],w:"The motion comes from the wrist; the finger simply travels with it."},
4:"Practise the motion for one minute without the bow, then one minute with it."}},

"violin-8":{title:"A complete melody",goal:"Play a familiar tune from beginning to end",b:{
0:"Now everything comes together: the finger pattern, the bow division and the crossings.",
1:"Melody for two strings",
2:"Play it first without the bow — just plucking — to check that the fingers are in place. Then add the bow.",
3:{q:"What is worth checking before you add the bow?",o:["The volume","That the fingers are in the right place","The tempo","Your head position"],w:"Plucking isolates the left hand and exposes inaccuracy straight away."},
4:"Play the melody five times — twice plucked and three times bowed."}},

"violin-9":{title:"Wrapping up, and what comes next",goal:"Play with confidence and know where to go",b:{
0:"You have finished the foundations: holding the instrument, open strings, the finger pattern, bow division and crossings. Everything else is built on this.",
1:"What comes next: <b>second position</b> (moving the whole hand up the neck), <b>playing on two strings at once</b>, and <b>reading music</b> while you play.",
2:"Closing piece",
3:"<b>The classic second-year mistake:</b> speeding up before the tone is clean. Play everything with a slow metronome — and you will overtake whoever rushed.",
4:{q:"What is the natural next step on the violin?",o:["Playing faster","Second position and reading music","Switching instruments","Giving up the bow"],w:"Second position opens the rest of the neck, and reading music opens the repertoire."},
5:"Play the closing piece and record yourself. Listening back is the most honest teacher there is."}},

/* ===================== HARMONICA ===================== */
"harmonica-1":{title:"Blow and draw",goal:"Produce a clean tone from a single hole",b:{
0:"The harmonica is the only instrument where <b>both blowing and drawing produce a note</b> — and two different notes from the same hole.",
1:"A harmonica in C has ten holes and twenty reeds. The fourth hole is the starting point of almost every tune.",
3:["Hold the harmonica between your thumb and index finger, numbers facing up.","Purse your lips as if you were saying 'oo'.","Blow gently into hole four — that is C.","Now draw from exactly the same hole — that is D."],
4:"Several notes coming out at once? Your mouth is too open. Narrow your lips until a single clean note remains.",
5:{q:"What makes the harmonica different from other wind instruments?",o:["It has no high notes","Both blowing and drawing produce a note","It has to be retuned every time","You have to press buttons"],w:"Each hole holds two reeds — one for blowing and one for drawing."},
6:"Switch between blowing and drawing on hole 4, twenty times, with a clean tone."}},

"harmonica-2":{title:"Four holes — your first tune",goal:"Play a familiar melody",b:{
0:"Holes 4 to 7 hold a complete C major scale. Almost every folk song lives right there.",
2:"Look at the arrow above the diagram: pointing up means blow, pointing down means draw. That is all you need to remember.",
3:"The scale on the harmonica",
4:{q:"Which holes hold a complete C major scale?",o:["1 to 4","4 to 7","7 to 10","All of them"],w:"Only holes 4 to 7 give the full scale without bending."},
5:"Play the scale up and down ten times."}},

"harmonica-3":{title:"Your first song",goal:"Play a whole tune from memory",b:{
0:"Now you put it together. This tune uses only holes 4 to 6.",
2:"Melody for four holes",
3:"Try playing without looking at the numbers. On a harmonica the ear finds the hole faster than the eye.",
4:{q:"What is the best way to learn a tune on the harmonica?",o:["Memorising the hole numbers","Listening and searching by ear","Counting breaths","Reading music"],w:"The harmonica was built for playing by ear — that is its strength."},
5:"Play the melody three times with your eyes closed."}},

"harmonica-4":{title:"Playing a single hole",goal:"Produce one clean note with no neighbours",b:{
0:"Until now you may have heard several notes together. Now you learn to bring out <b>one note only</b> — and that is what turns noise into a melody.",
1:["Place the harmonica deeper into your lips, as if you were drinking.","Your tongue rests low and relaxed.","Narrow the opening of your lips until a single note sounds.","Test it: blow on hole 4 — if you hear a chord, your lips are too wide."],
3:"A second method, <b>tongue blocking</b>: cover three holes with your mouth and block two of them with your tongue. That leaves one hole open.",
4:{q:"Why do several notes sound at once?",o:["The harmonica is broken","The lip opening is too wide","You are blowing too hard","The holes are blocked"],w:"A wide mouth covers several holes at the same time."},
5:"Play holes 4, 5 and 6 blowing — each one separately and cleanly, twenty times."}},

"harmonica-5":{title:"The second octave",goal:"Extend your range to holes 1 through 7",b:{
0:"The low holes (1–3) give the deep notes, and the high ones (7–10) the sharp ones. Most tunes live in the middle.",
2:"Notice something odd: from hole 7 upwards <b>the draw is lower than the blow</b> — the opposite of the low holes. That is a feature of the tuning, not a fault.",
3:"Full range",
4:{q:"What changes from hole 7 upwards?",o:["Nothing changes","The draw is lower than the blow","You cannot draw","The tone is weaker"],w:"It is a built-in reversal in Richter tuning."},
5:"Play every blow note from hole 1 to 10 and back."}},

"harmonica-6":{title:"Rhythm and breathing",goal:"Play in steady time without running out of air",b:{
0:"On a harmonica your breath is both the sound and the fuel. Play too long in one direction and you will run out.",
1:"Alternating blow and draw",
2:"<b>The rule:</b> if a tune asks for three blows in a row, look for a place to change direction. Usually the same note is available in another hole.",
3:"Breathing exercise",
4:{q:"What do you do when you run out of air mid-phrase?",o:["Blow harder","Look for a place to change direction","Stop playing","Play faster"],w:"Changing direction is how a harmonica breathes."},
5:"Play the exercise for two minutes straight without getting out of breath."}},

"harmonica-7":{title:"Chords and tremolo",goal:"Use several holes together on purpose",b:{
0:"Until now we fought against the neighbouring notes. Now you <b>use them deliberately</b>: several holes together give a full chord.",
1:"Blowing holes 1–4 together gives a C major chord. Drawing holes 1–4 gives a G chord.",
2:"<b>Tremolo:</b> shake the hand holding the harmonica in front of your mouth. It makes the note waver and sounds like a train.",
3:"Chord accompaniment",
4:{q:"Which chord comes from blowing holes 1 to 4?",o:["G major","C major","A minor","D major"],w:"The blow notes are built from the notes of a C chord — C, E and G."},
5:"Play a chord accompaniment to a song you know, for two minutes."}},

"harmonica-8":{title:"A whole song",goal:"Play a familiar tune from start to finish",b:{
0:"Now you combine it all: single holes, rhythm and planned breathing.",
2:"A complete melody",
3:"Mark for yourself in advance where you blow and where you draw. Planning ahead saves you from running out of air.",
4:{q:"What helps you avoid running out of breath?",o:["Playing quietly","Planning in advance when to blow and when to draw","Playing fast","Shortening the song"],w:"Planning the direction of the air is the core skill of the harmonica."},
5:"Play the melody five times in a row without stopping."}},

"harmonica-9":{title:"Wrapping up, and what comes next",goal:"Know what you learned and where to go",b:{
0:"You have finished the foundations: blowing and drawing, single holes, the full range, rhythm, chords and a whole song.",
1:"What comes next: <b>bending</b> — changing the pitch with your tongue, which lets you play the notes that are not in the tuning. That is what gives the harmonica its bluesy sound.",
2:"Closing piece",
3:"Bending takes months, not days. Do not work on it before your single notes are completely clean.",
4:{q:"What makes bending possible?",o:["A different harmonica","Changing the shape of your mouth and tongue","Blowing hard","Replacing the reeds"],w:"The shape of the mouth cavity changes the pitch."},
5:"Play the closing piece and record yourself."}},

/* ===================== GLOCKENSPIEL ===================== */
"glock-1":{title:"A first strike",goal:"Produce a clean tone and learn the layout",b:{
0:"The glockenspiel is the easiest melodic instrument to start on: <b>nothing to tune, no fingering, and no breath</b>. You strike — and the right note comes out.",
1:"The bars are laid out exactly like piano keys: a long row of white ones, and above them groups of two and three.",
2:"Strike the marked bar — this is C",
3:["Hold the mallet loosely, between your thumb and index finger.","Strike the centre of the bar — not the end.","Lift the mallet immediately after the strike. A mallet left on the bar chokes the note.","A light strike is enough. The instrument does the work."],
4:"A dull sound? The mallet is staying on the bar. Think of bouncing, not pressing.",
5:{q:"Where do you strike the bar?",o:["At the end","In the centre","Near the screw","It makes no difference"],w:"The centre gives the fullest tone. The ends sound dull."},
6:"Strike every white bar once, from low to high, with a clean tone."}},

"glock-2":{title:"The scale and your first tune",goal:"Play a familiar melody",b:{
0:"A C major scale is simply all the white bars in a row. That is all you need for your first tune.",
1:"C major scale",
2:"A first melody",
3:"<b>Alternating hands:</b> right-left-right-left. It doubles your speed with no extra effort.",
4:{q:"What is the advantage of alternating hands?",o:["A louder sound","You can play faster with no extra effort","The bars wear out less","There is no advantage"],w:"One hand has to travel back; two hands alternate continuously."},
5:"Play the melody with alternating hands, five times."}},

"glock-3":{title:"Rhythm and dynamics",goal:"Play at different volumes and in time",b:{
0:"On a glockenspiel the note is short, so <b>timing and volume</b> are all the expression you have. That is what turns unremarkable playing into remarkable playing.",
1:"Basic rhythm",
2:"Try playing the same melody once strongly and once gently. The difference is bigger than it seems.",
3:"The same melody, a different rhythm",
4:{q:"What creates expression on a glockenspiel?",o:["Note length","Volume and timing","Tuning the bars","The number of mallets"],w:"The note is short and fixed, so volume and timing are your tools."},
5:"Play the melody three times: softly, moderately and loudly."}},

"glock-4":{title:"Two hands and jumps",goal:"Play a passage that moves between areas",b:{
0:"On a glockenspiel the hand does not stay put. <b>The eye leads — the hand follows</b>, and that is a skill learned separately.",
1:"Three anchor points — C, G and high C",
2:["Find the three anchor points on the instrument.","Strike C and then G, without looking at the mallet.","Add the high C.","Now play all three in sequence at metronome 60."],
3:"Look at the <b>next</b> bar while you are striking the current one. That is the secret of every mallet player.",
4:{q:"Where do you look while playing?",o:["At the mallet","At the next bar","Only at the music","It makes no difference"],w:"The eye runs ahead of the hand, just as in reading."},
5:"Play C-G-C twenty times without missing."}},

"glock-5":{title:"The upper bars",goal:"Play in a scale with a sharp",b:{
0:"The upper bars are the semitones — exactly like the black keys on a piano, and in the same groups of two and three.",
1:"G major scale — watch out for the F sharp",
2:"The upper bars are narrower. Aim for the centre, or the mallet will slip off.",
3:{q:"How are the upper bars arranged?",o:["In an even row","In groups of two and three","Randomly","There are no upper bars"],w:"Exactly like the black keys of a piano."},
4:"Play a G major scale up and down, five times."}},

"glock-6":{title:"More complex rhythm",goal:"Play eighth notes and dotted notes",b:{
0:"The note on a glockenspiel is short, so <b>accuracy in time</b> is heard immediately. Here you learn to divide the beat.",
1:"Eighth notes",
2:"A melody in eighths",
3:"Count out loud: 'one-and-two-and'. Counting aloud is more accurate than counting in your head.",
4:{q:"What helps with rhythmic accuracy?",o:["Playing loudly","Counting out loud","Playing fast","Watching your hands"],w:"Counting aloud connects your body to time."},
5:"Play the melody at metronome 70, then at 85."}},

"glock-7":{title:"Two voices",goal:"Play a melody and an accompaniment together",b:{
0:"Two hands means two voices. One hand plays the melody, the other adds an anchor note.",
1:"A C chord — the three notes together",
2:"A melody with an anchor",
3:"Start with the second hand striking only on the first beat of each bar. That is already enough to sound full.",
4:{q:"What is the simplest way to add a second voice?",o:["Play everything twice","An anchor note on the first beat","Play faster","Add a third mallet"],w:"A single anchor per bar already creates the feel of accompaniment."},
5:"Play the melody with an anchor, five times."}},

"glock-8":{title:"A piece to perform",goal:"Play a complete piece with confidence",b:{
0:"Now you combine it all: jumps, upper bars, rhythm and two voices.",
1:"A piece to perform",
2:"Play the piece in three separate sections, and only then join them. That is how professionals learn.",
3:{q:"How do you learn a long piece?",o:["Play it over and over","Split it into sections and then join them","Play only the beginning","Speed up gradually"],w:"Splitting it saves time and prevents mistakes from being baked in."},
4:"Play the piece three times in a row without stopping."}},

"glock-9":{title:"Wrapping up, and what comes next",goal:"Know what you learned and where to go",b:{
0:"You have finished: a clean strike, the scale, jumps, the upper bars, rhythm and two voices.",
1:"What comes next: <b>xylophone and marimba</b> (wooden bars, a warmer tone), <b>four mallets</b> in two hands, and <b>reading music</b> while you play.",
2:"Closing piece",
3:"The glockenspiel is an excellent gateway to every melodic percussion instrument. The skill transfers almost entirely.",
4:{q:"What is the difference between a glockenspiel and a xylophone?",o:["The size","Metal bars versus wooden bars","The number of bars","The tuning"],w:"Metal gives a bright, long tone; wood gives a warm, short one."},
5:"Play the closing piece and record yourself."}},

/* ===================== MANDOLIN ===================== */
"mandolin-1":{title:"Getting to know it, and the open strings",goal:"Produce a clean tone and learn the tuning",b:{
0:"The mandolin is tuned <b>exactly like a violin</b> — G, D, A, E — but it has frets. Every note sits in a fixed place, and nothing has to be found by ear.",
2:["Hold the pick between your thumb and index finger, by the tip only.","Pick down and up alternately — that is the basic motion.","Left hand: press just behind the fret, not on top of it.","Play each open pair of strings four times."],
3:"Every note has two strings side by side. That is what gives the mandolin its ringing sound.",
4:{q:"How does a mandolin differ from a violin?",o:["In its tuning","In having frets","In the number of strings","Only in size"],w:"The tuning is identical; the frets are the difference, and they are what makes it easier for beginners."},
5:"Pick each open string eight times, down and up."}},

"mandolin-2":{title:"First notes on the neck",goal:"Play your first scale",b:{
0:"Thanks to the frets, finding a note on a mandolin is a matter of counting — not of listening.",
2:"Five notes",
3:"One finger per fret — that is the rule that saves you years of corrections.",
4:{q:"How do you find a note on a mandolin?",o:["By ear only","By the fret number","By the colour of the string","You cannot be certain"],w:"The frets fix the pitch exactly."},
5:"Play the five notes up and down, ten times."}},

"mandolin-3":{title:"A first melody",goal:"Play a whole tune",b:{
0:"Now you combine them: open strings, notes on the neck, and a steady picking hand.",
2:"A melody for mandolin",
3:"<b>Tremolo:</b> fast repeated picking on the same note. It is the sound everyone recognises as a mandolin — but only once your ordinary picking is steady.",
4:{q:"What is tremolo?",o:["A fast chord change","Fast repeated picking on the same note","Playing on two strings","Slowing down at the end"],w:"It is the signature technique of the mandolin."},
5:"Play the melody five times with even picking."}},

"mandolin-4":{title:"Your first chord",goal:"Play a G chord and change to it",b:{
0:"On a mandolin a chord is <b>two fingers only</b> in most cases — far fewer than a guitar needs.",
2:["Finger 1 on the A string, fret 2.","Finger 2 on the E string, fret 3.","The other two strings stay open.","Pick downwards across all four pairs."],
3:"Press just behind the fret, not on it. That is where the least force is needed.",
4:{q:"How many fingers does a basic mandolin chord need?",o:["Four","Two","Three","None"],w:"Two are enough for most open chords."},
5:"Switch between the G chord and open strings, twenty times."}},

"mandolin-5":{title:"Two chords and a song",goal:"Accompany a whole song with two chords",b:{
0:"With two chords you can already accompany hundreds of songs. The change between them is the whole practice.",
1:"Down-up picking",
2:"<b>The rule of the change:</b> lift your fingers a moment before the beat, not on it. That way there is no hole in the rhythm.",
3:"Accompaniment exercise",
4:{q:"When do you lift your fingers during a chord change?",o:["Exactly on the beat","A moment before the beat","After the beat","It makes no difference"],w:"Lifting early keeps the rhythm continuous."},
5:"Alternate between the two chords for two minutes without stopping."}},

"mandolin-6":{title:"Tremolo",goal:"Produce a sustained sound with repeated picking",b:{
0:"A mandolin has no long note — it dies away immediately. <b>Tremolo</b> is the answer: fast repeated picking that creates the illusion of a sustained tone.",
1:["Hold the pick very loosely — that is the key.","Move from the wrist, not the elbow.","Start slow and even, and only then speed up.","Play one note in tremolo for four seconds."],
2:"A tight grip on the pick means a jumpy tremolo. The softer the grip, the smoother the sound.",
3:"A long note in tremolo",
4:{q:"What matters most in tremolo?",o:["Speed","A relaxed grip","Heavy pressure","A thick pick"],w:"Relaxation is what allows the speed; tension prevents it."},
5:"Practise tremolo on a single note, one minute per string."}},

"mandolin-7":{title:"Scales across two octaves",goal:"Extend your range along the neck",b:{
0:"Because the tuning is in fifths, the same finger pattern repeats on every pair of strings. You learn it once — and play it everywhere.",
2:"G scale across two octaves",
3:"The pattern you learned on the G string works in exactly the same way on the D string. That is the great saving of tuning in fifths.",
4:{q:"What is the advantage of tuning in fifths?",o:["A louder sound","The same pattern repeats on every pair of strings","It is easier to tune","Fewer strings"],w:"The pattern is preserved, so you only ever learn it once."},
5:"Play the scale across two octaves, ten times."}},

"mandolin-8":{title:"Melody with accompaniment",goal:"Play a whole song and weave in chords",b:{
0:"Now you combine them: melody, chords and tremolo in the same piece.",
1:"A combined piece",
2:"Play the melody alone first, then the chords alone, and only then combine. Combining too early ruins both.",
3:{q:"How do you combine melody and accompaniment?",o:["Play them together from the start","Learn each separately and then combine","Give up one of them","Play very slowly"],w:"Separate first, combine afterwards — that is the fast route."},
4:"Play the combined piece five times."}},

"mandolin-9":{title:"Wrapping up, and what comes next",goal:"Know what you learned and where to go",b:{
0:"You have finished: open strings, notes on the neck, chords, tremolo and scales across two octaves.",
1:"What comes next: <b>bluegrass style</b> (fast, precise picking), <b>closed chords</b> that move along the neck, and <b>improvising</b> on the scales you have learned.",
2:"Closing piece",
3:"The mandolin shares its tuning with the violin — everything you learned here transfers almost entirely if you ever want to try it.",
4:{q:"Which instrument shares the mandolin's tuning?",o:["Guitar","Violin","Bass","Ukulele"],w:"Both are tuned G-D-A-E."},
5:"Play the closing piece and record yourself."}}

});
